# Publish to Railway (Spodazo Books)

Railway production (`https://books.spodazo.com`) deploys from **`main` only**.

## Before the first edit

Check out `main` and pull `origin/main`. Stay on `main` for the whole job. Do not create a feature branch. A new chat must switch to `main` itself before editing so every chat in this repo ends up on `main`.

## Finish in the same turn

A change is not done until `git push origin main`.

1. Implement and verify on `main`.
2. Commit on `main` when the change is complete.
3. `git push origin main` in that same turn. No pull request. No merge step.
4. Confirm the Railway / GitHub deployment for that commit is pending or succeeded.
5. Say whether it is live yet, and give the commit short SHA.

Do not leave the commit only on local `main`. Do not invent a feature branch. Do not ask for a separate publish step before the push.

## When they say yes / publish / pp

If the push has not happened yet, commit any remaining work and `git push origin main`. No extra confirmation.

## Never

- Force-push `main`.
- Update git config.
- Skip hooks.
- Open a pull request or merge a pull request as the normal publish path.
- Say or imply changes are “published” / “live” before `main` is pushed and the deploy for that commit has succeeded.
- Stop at a local commit.
- Leave them unsure whether production updated — always state live vs not live.
